outputs.exact_statement:
The Hodge structures $P_H^\pm$ are polarizable, and $$\label{cliff:multiplication}
 m^Q:H(1)\longrightarrow\operatorname{Hom}(P_H^+,P_H^-),
 \qquad x\longmapsto L_x$$ is a rational Hodge embedding. The parity spaces are rationally Hodge-isomorphic by right multiplication by any fixed invertible odd Clifford element. On a sufficiently small period chart, their Hodge filtrations vary holomorphically and admit fixed rational polarizations.

There are complex multiplicity spaces $M,N_+,N_-$ and a trivial $\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}$-module $M_0$ such that $$\label{gen:spin-decompositions}
 U_{\mathbb C}=(\Delta\otimes M)\oplus M_0,
 \qquad W^\pm_{\mathbb C}=\Delta\otimes N_\pm,
 \qquad \dim N_\pm=2^{10}.$$ Every rational $\mathfrak g$-equivariant map $U\to W^\pm$ is a weight-one Hodge map. Consequently $$\label{gen:hom-base-change}
 \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{HS}}(U,W^\pm)\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb C
 =\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}}
       (U_{\mathbb C},W^\pm_{\mathbb C})
 \simeq\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb C}(M,N_\pm).$$
